{"title":"Career roadmap · Biomedical Engineer","role":"Biomedical Engineer","summary":"A Biomedical Engineer applies engineering, electronics, computing, materials science and biology to develop, test, maintain and improve medical devices, diagnostic systems, rehabilitation technologies, biosensors and healthcare technologies.","facts":{},"about":[],"intro":[],"horizons":[],"sections":[{"heading":"Overview","kind":"other","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The field spans medical instrumentation, biomaterials, medical imaging, biosignal processing, biomechanics, rehabilitation engineering, clinical engineering and healthcare AI."},{"type":"note","label":"Career-route check","text":"This is a directly aligned career path for Biomedical Engineering graduates. Entry-level opportunities commonly include Biomedical Engineer, Clinical Engineer, Medical Equipment Engineer, Application Engineer, Service Engineer, R&D Engineer and Medical Device Engineer."},{"type":"note","label":"Timeline assumption","text":"4-year B.Tech/B.E. starting in 2026 → graduation around 2030."}]},{"heading":"Career horizons","kind":"horizons","blocks":[{"type":"table","header":["Period","Career focus"],"rows":[[{"text":"2026–2030"},{"text":"Build electronics, programming, anatomy/physiology, instrumentation and biomedical-device fundamentals."}],[{"text":"2030–2035"},{"text":"Gain experience in medical devices, clinical engineering, equipment service, applications, validation or R&D."}],[{"text":"2035–2040"},{"text":"Specialise in medical devices, imaging, biomaterials, biosignals, robotics, regulatory affairs or healthcare AI."}],[{"text":"2040–2046"},{"text":"Progress toward Senior/Principal Biomedical Engineer, Clinical Engineering Manager, R&D Lead, Medical Device Architect or healthcare-technology leadership."}]]}]},{"heading":"What to build each year","kind":"build","blocks":[{"type":"table","header":["When","Build this","Learn from","Proof of progress","Gap it moves"],"rows":[[{"text":"Year 1 S